<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<CycloneDDS xmlns="https://cdds.io/config" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="https://cdds.io/config https://raw.githubusercontent.com/eclipse-cyclonedds/cyclonedds/master/etc/cyclonedds.xsd">
  <Domain Id="any">
    <General>
      <Interfaces>
        <NetworkInterface autodetermine="false" name="lo" priority="default" multicast="default" />
      </Interfaces>
      <AllowMulticast>default</AllowMulticast>
      <MaxMessageSize>65500B</MaxMessageSize>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <ParticipantIndex>none</ParticipantIndex>
    </Discovery>
    <SharedMemory>
      <!--
        Iceoryx zero-copy transport. DISABLED, after it was tried and measured.
        Two independent findings, either of which is disqualifying on its own.

        1. It does not fit. RouDi died with PORT_POOL__PUBLISHERLIST_OVERFLOW
           the first time the full stack came up. iceoryx caps publisher ports
           at IOX_MAX_PUBLISHERS = 512, and that constant is COMPILE-TIME:
           iceoryx_posh_deployment.hpp is autogenerated and says so - the only
           way to raise it is to rebuild iceoryx from source with
           -DIOX_MAX_PUBLISHERS=N. There is no roudi.toml key for it;
           roudi.toml sizes mempools only.

        2. Ports are consumed by writers that can never benefit. Every overflow
           in the RouDi log named the same service - ros_discovery_info, whose
           type is rmw_dds_common::msg::dds_::ParticipantEntitiesInfo_. That
           type is full of strings, so it is not loanable and gets nothing from
           shared memory, yet Cyclone still registers an iceoryx publisher port
           for it. Port consumption therefore scales with the TOTAL number of
           DDS writers, while the benefit reaches only fixed-size types.

        And the benefit was already near zero here. Measured with
        can_loan_messages(), RouDi up and SHM on:

            sensor_msgs/PointCloud2  no     geometry_msgs/Twist  YES
            sensor_msgs/Image        no     std_msgs/UInt64      YES
            sensor_msgs/Imu          no

        Any message carrying a std_msgs/Header is excluded, because frame_id is
        a string. That is every sensor and perception topic on this vehicle.

        The failure mode is a hard crash, not a fallback: iceoryx aborts with
        EXPECTS_ENSURES_FAILED and the node dies with "terminate called without
        an active exception".

        To re-enable after rebuilding iceoryx with a raised port limit, flip the
        value below and start iox-roudi.service. The guards in
        scripts/iceoryx/ensure_roudi.sh and scripts/env.sh read this very
        element, so they follow automatically - do not write the enabled form of
        this tag inside a comment, or that grep will match it.
      -->
      <Enable>false</Enable>
      <LogLevel>warn</LogLevel>
    </SharedMemory>
    <Internal>
      <!--
        Sized against a measured drop rate, not a guess. With the stack up,
        /proc/net/snmp showed 1308 UDP RcvbufErrors/s while lo carried 205k
        packets/s. Every one of those drops costs a NACK and a retransmit that
        CycloneDDS schedules on its "tev" thread, which `just profile perf`
        measured as the single hottest thread on the machine. Raising the
        socket buffer is what breaks that feedback loop.

        max= as well as min=: with only min= set, Cyclone asks for the size and
        accepts whatever the kernel gives back, so the request silently lands on
        net.core.rmem_default (208 kB here) unless the sysctl is also raised.
        See setup.sh cyclonedds-sysctl.
      -->
      <SocketReceiveBufferSize min="16MB" max="16MB"/>
      <!--
        Cyclone's default heartbeat floor is 5 ms. Every reliable writer gets one,
        and each heartbeat draws AckNacks back from every matched reader, so the
        cost grows with the square of the participant count. 100 ms is still far
        inside the deadline of anything here - a lost sample is recovered on the
        next heartbeat, not this one - and it takes the bulk of the periodic work
        off tev.
      -->
      <HeartbeatInterval min="100ms" minsched="200ms" max="8s"/>
      <Watermarks>
        <WhcHigh>500kB</WhcHigh>
      </Watermarks>
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
